  "worked_solution": "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form. \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $5{y}_{1}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the given curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the two parallel lines ${y}_{1}=\\sqrt{2}$ and ${y}_{1}=-\\sqrt{2}.$",
